  "query_preview": "/Thursday through Monday/...\nIssued 327 AM MST MON NOV 23 2026\nHigh pressure will keep temperatures warm and snow levels near \n6500-7500 ft across the region on Friday and Saturday, but \nPacific moisture will continue to bring pulses of precipitation \ndespite ridging. The ridge will begin to weaken on Saturday, \nwith another closed low/upper level trough moving into the \nregion on Sunday. This will likely bring cooler temperatures, \nlower snow levels, and another push of widespread precipitation \nto the area. Accumulations look to generally be minimal with \neach round of precipitation, but the length of time of this wet\nperiod will bring many sites to near record high precipitation \namounts for November. Several SNOTEL sites at higher locations \nin the Steens, Owyhee, and Boise Mountains are recording near \nrecord snow depth for November. For example, the station at \nBogus Basin measured 20 inches for the current snow depth (near\n95th percentile) while the median snow depth for late November \nis 9 inches, more than double the typical snow depth for early \nwinter.",
